{"text": " why doesn't the width of your own car appear anywhere in the mathematics of parallel parking? Look at mathematician Simon Blackburn's exact parking formula. It uses your turning radius, your wheelbase, your front overhang, and the width of the parked car in front of you. Your own car's width? Completely missing. Here is why. In a single smooth sweep on a wide road, the only pinch point is your curbside front corner barely missing the car in front. Making your own car wider pushes the outside edge closer to your turning center without changing that front corner's path at all. In real life, of course, a wider car still steals room on narrow streets or when you have to shuffle back and forth.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.78, "text": " why doesn't the width of your own car appear anywhere in the mathematics of parallel parking?", "tokens": [50363, 1521, 1595, 470, 262, 9647, 286, 534, 898, 1097, 1656, 6609, 287, 262, 19473, 286, 10730, 7647, 30, 50631], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.080182000865107, "compression_ratio": 1.7413127413127414, "no_speech_prob": 0.0005235805292613804, "words": [{"word": " why", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.34, "probability": 0.08972694724798203}, {"word": " doesn't", "start": 0.34, "end": 0.66, "probability": 0.9881832599639893}, {"word": " the", "start": 0.66, "end": 0.8, "probability": 0.9974513649940491}, {"word": " width", "start": 0.8, "end": 1.06, "probability": 0.9963297247886658}, {"word": " of", "start": 1.06, "end": 1.24, "probability": 0.995703399181366}, {"word": " your", "start": 1.24, "end": 1.38, "probability": 0.9915319085121155}, {"word": " own", "start": 1.38, "end": 1.64, "probability": 0.9962453246116638}, {"word": " car", "start": 1.64, "end": 1.98, "probability": 0.9982514977455139}, {"word": " appear", "start": 1.98, "end": 2.34, "probability": 0.9966160655021667}, {"word": " anywhere", "start": 2.34, "end": 2.88, "probability": 0.9893485307693481}, {"word": " in", "start": 2.